This paper presents the development of an automotive retrofit system for real-time data monitoring and vehicle diagnostics, utilizing the OBD-II protocol and the ESP32 microcontroller. The central problem lies in the limited information provided by instrument clusters in early 2000s vehicles, which often omit critical data such as precise engine temperature, RPM, and efficiency indicators, hindering preventive maintenance and early fault detection. The proposed solution consists of an embedded device that communicates via Bluetooth Low Energy (BLE) with an ELM327 adapter, which interfaces with the Electronic Control Unit (ECU), processing parameters based on the SAE J1979 standard. The system features a dual Human-Machine Interface (HMI): a 16x2 LCD display for quick reference during driving and a web dashboard accessible via smartphone for advanced diagnostics, including reading Diagnostic Trouble Codes (DTCs), fuel consumption calculation, and safety alerts (overheating and knock detection). Partial results demonstrate the technical feasibility of a low-cost solution that promotes the democratization of automotive telemetry, providing greater operational safety and longevity to legacy vehicles. 

Keywords: Automotive Telemetry. OBD-II. ESP32. Retrofit. Predictive Maintenance. 
